From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mpereiraesaa <manuelpereiralds@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 17:43:02 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PC5: a page made read-only beside a writable one faults again
 (Wine patch 0974)

prospero-win 91615eb (#718), after the v0.2.0 PC5 builds on, picked onto
PC5's patches. The Wine patch is rebased onto PC5's series.

With 16 KiB host pages a host page took the union of its four Windows
pages' protections, so a page made read-only next to a writable one
stayed writable and a write to it raised nothing. DuckStation protects
the 4 KiB pages holding the code it recompiled and drops that code on
the fault; on the console it never came, Dino Crisis 2 loaded new code
over old and the emulator jumped into garbage (black screen, 0 fps).

Outside images, a committed read-only or inaccessible page now takes
writing, or all access, away from its host page. An access that faults
only because of such a neighbour is single-stepped with the host page
open for that one instruction. After 256 of those writes in one host
page, the next is reported as a write to its read-only page instead:
the console's steps are expensive (DuckStation took twelve thousand a
second beside one code page), and a program that protects pages to see
them change drops the protection. The count restarts after a report,
so a program that keeps the page read-only is not caught in a loop.

WINE_HOST_PAGE_SIZE=16384 makes the PC's Wine keep 16 KiB host pages:
tests/test_wine_subpage_protect.py runs its program both ways, in
64-bit and WoW64 processes.

